  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the sources satisfy `requirements.configuration.user_location`.\n\n**Implementation (`src/config.rs`)**\n- `user_config_path_from` (about lines 566–580) does what the requirement says on each platform:\n  - On Unix, an absolute `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` gives `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/habeas/config.toml`.\n  - If `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is absent, empty or relative, it falls back to `$HOME/.config/habeas/config.toml`. An empty path is not absolute, so the `absolute_directory` filter covers the \"nonempty\" condition.\n  - On Windows, it uses `%APPDATA%/habeas/config.toml`.\n- `required_directory` returns an `io::Error` of kind `NotFound` when the base directory can't be determined. Its message names the variable and says it \"must be an absolute, nonempty path\". This covers the \"fail with an explanatory diagnostic\" obligation.\n- `user_config_path` uses `ConfigPlatform::current()` with the real environment.\n- Nothing else in `src` derives a user config path or reads `HOME`, `XDG_*` or `APPDATA` except the state-root code. That code is separately marked as an implementation of `state_location`.\n- The location depends only on the platform and environment, never on the current project or on project settings. The one real caller, `resolve_authoring_from` (about line 817), gets the path from `user_config_path()?`. A failure there propagates as an error.\n- All the relevant helpers (`ConfigPlatform::current`, `user_config_path`, `user_config_path_from`, `absolute_directory`, `required_directory`) carry `{impl}` markers.\n- The extra behavior in these helpers is that a relative `HOME` or `APPDATA` is rejected. That is a conservative reading of \"cannot be determined\" and doesn't conflict with the requirement.\n\n**Tests (`tests/configuration.rs`, `locations_use_absolute_xdg_values_and_platform_fallbacks`)**\n- Absolute `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is used as the base.\n- An empty or relative `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` falls back to `$HOME/.config/habeas/config.toml`.\n- Windows with `APPDATA` gives `<APPDATA>/habeas/config.toml`.\n- With no environment variables, Unix returns an error.\n- The test does not cover a relative `HOME` or `APPDATA`, or the error message text. These are minor gaps.\n- The test doesn't need to check that the location is independent of the project. The path function takes no project argument, so that property follows from its signature.\n- The tests inject a lookup function, so they run on any platform.\n\n**Other points**\n- The rest of the requirement is about workspace settings living in `habeas.toml`. That belongs to `workspace_location`, which the requirement only cites.\n- `md/reference.md` documents the same location behavior.",
